{"name":"Deep Well","hex":"#33313b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"well","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Accurately, competently, satisfactorily.","Completely, fully."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["In good health.","Highly satisfactory"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/well"}],"entries":[{"title":"Well","blocks":[{"text":"A well is an excavation or structure created on the earth by digging, driving, or drilling to access liquid resources, usually water. The oldest and most common kind of well is a water well, to access groundwater in underground aquifers. The well water is drawn up by a pump, or using containers, such as buckets that are raised mechanically or by hand. Water can also be injected back into the aquifer through the well. Wells were first constructed at least eight thousand years ago and historically vary in construction from a sediment of a dry watercourse to the qanats of Iran, and the stepwells and sakiehs of India. Placing a lining in the well shaft helps create stability, and linings of wood or wickerwork date back at least as far as the Iron Age."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Faryab-_village_dug_well.JPG/330px-Faryab-_village_dug_well.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Well"},{"title":"Deepwater drilling","blocks":[{"text":"Deepwater drilling, or deep well drilling, is the process of creating holes in the Earth's crust using a drilling rig for oil extraction under the deep sea. There are approximately 3400 deepwater wells in the Gulf of Mexico with depths greater than 150 meters."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Discoverer-inspiraton-fleet.jpg/330px-Discoverer-inspiraton-fleet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deepwater_drilling"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Well-cistern.jpg/500px-Well-cistern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Well-cistern.jpg/1280px-Well-cistern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Well-cistern.jpg/500px-Well-cistern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The difference between a well and a cistern is in the source of the water: a cistern collects rainwater whereas a well draws from groundwater.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWell-cistern.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg/500px-Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg/1280px-Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg/500px-Tunis1960-150_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Camel drawing water from a well, Djerba island, Tunisia, 1960","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATunis1960-150_hg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wi